solnix T2.1 — fix the intermittent ~50% early-kernel boot hang on EC2 Nitro/KVM.

SYMPTOM: boot reaches "TSC calibrated using VMware; freq is ~2.9 GHz" then hangs
(~50-75% of boots), no further console output, no panic.

ROOT CAUSE: after a hypervisor TSC-frequency source ("VMware", which also covers
KVM/VirtualBox/ACRN via get_hwenv() & HW_SUPPORTS_FREQ) wins TSC calibration,
tsc_calibrate() calls tsc_pit_also() (timestamp.c) which runs a *diagnostic* PIT
(legacy 8254) calibration purely to record what the PIT would have measured, for
the APIC code. That PIT run does port I/O to the emulated 8254 (freq_tsc_pit in
locore.S + pit_sanity_check in tscc_pit.c). On EC2 Nitro/KVM the emulated PIT is
unreliable: the calibration loop's refine-and-retry can spin for a
pathologically long (though technically bounded) time, presenting as a boot
hang. This runs on the boot CPU BEFORE /etc/system is read (startup_tsc precedes
startup_modules), so `set pit_is_broken=1` in /etc/system is TOO LATE, and it is
NOT a boot property either -- so the ONLY early knob is a source default.

Confirmed single-CPU (1-vCPU instances still hung ~75%), ruling out an MP/AP
startup race; and the hypervisor detection is correct (VMware/KVM source is the
right one). The PIT-also diagnostic is the culprit.

FIX: default pit_is_broken = 1. tscc_pit.c already documents that pit_is_broken
may be set to make the system avoid the PIT entirely; we make that the default.
On a cloud VM the PIT diagnostic is worthless (the hypervisor freq source always
wins), so skipping it loses nothing and removes the hang.

MEASURED: m6i.large batch success (booted to multi-user-server):
  BEFORE: 4/10 (40%)      AFTER: 20/20 (100%)   -- see docs/design-make-be.md #10

--- a/usr/src/uts/i86pc/os/tscc_pit.c
+++ b/usr/src/uts/i86pc/os/tscc_pit.c
@@ -48,7 +48,13 @@
  * history built up using the alternative calibration sources.
  */
 uint64_t pit_tsc_hz;
-int pit_is_broken;
+/*
+ * solnix T2.1: default the PIT to "broken" so the diagnostic PIT calibration in
+ * tsc_pit_also() never runs. On EC2 Nitro/KVM the emulated 8254 makes that run
+ * spin ~50% of boots (hang at "TSC calibrated using VMware"). The hypervisor
+ * TSC-freq source wins anyway, so the PIT diagnostic is pure, hazardous overhead.
+ */
+int pit_is_broken = 1;
 
 /*
